    <string name="guide_picture">Picture for this chapter</string>
    <string name="guide_launcher_title">What a launcher is</string>
    <string name="guide_launcher_body">A launcher is the app you see when you press Home. It shows your apps and widgets and is where you start everything else. Android lets you choose your launcher, and Enyo is one.\n\nTo use Enyo, make it your home app: Enyo’s setup asks for this under Permissions. From then on, pressing Home always brings you back to Enyo.</string>
    <string name="guide_sections_title">Finding your way around</string>
    <string name="guide_sections_body">Enyo has five sections, named along the bottom: Today, Apps, Cards, Inbox and Quick. Swipe left or right, or tap a name, to move between them.\n\nPressing Home takes you to Cards. The magnifier at the top opens Search, and the gear opens Settings. With a keyboard, the keys 1 to 5 jump straight to a section.</string>
    <string name="guide_today_title">The Today view</string>
    <string name="guide_today_body">Today is your overview of the day. It holds widgets: small live pieces of information such as the clock, your next event, the weather, the battery, your connection and news.\n\nEnyo brings its own widgets, and you can add widgets from your other apps too.</string>
    <string name="guide_widgets_title">Arranging widgets</string>
    <string name="guide_widgets_body">Touch and hold an empty spot on Today to add a widget or to resize the ones you have.\n\nTouch and hold a widget to pick it up, then drag it to a new place. To remove it, drop it on Remove at the top. While resizing, drag a widget’s handle or use the buttons above it to make it taller, shorter, wider or narrower.</string>
    <string name="guide_apps_title">Your apps and the dock</string>
    <string name="guide_apps_body">Apps lists every app on your phone by name. Your messaging apps share one Messages tile, which asks which of them to open.\n\nTouch and hold an app for its actions: pin it to the dock, open its app info or uninstall it. The dock sits at the bottom of Cards and holds up to five apps you use most. At the top of the list, pull down to search.</string>
    <string name="guide_cards_title">App cards</string>
    <string name="guide_cards_body">Cards shows the apps you used recently, each as a card. Tap a card to go back to that app.\n\nTo close an app, swipe its card up. Clear all closes every card at once. Once card previews are set up, a card shows what the app looked like when you left it.</string>
    <string name="guide_stacks_title">Stacking cards</string>
    <string name="guide_stacks_body">Apps you use together can share a stack, like a travel app with its tickets. Touch and hold a card, drag it onto another card and let go: they become one stack of up to nine cards. Drop it beside a card instead to change the order.\n\nTap a stack to fan it out and pick a card. While a stack is fanned out, drag a card away from it to take it out again.</string>
    <string name="guide_search_title">Search</string>
    <string name="guide_search_body">Search finds apps, settings, contacts, calendar events, saved messages and files. Open it with the magnifier, by pulling down on a section, or by starting to type on a keyboard.\n\nYou can also write what you want done. “Message Alex that I’m on my way” prepares a message for you to check. Timers, alarms and the flashlight work the same way. Anything else can go to your assistant or the web.</string>
    <string name="guide_inbox_title">The unified inbox and chat heads</string>
    <string name="guide_inbox_body">Inbox gathers your conversations from all your messaging apps in one place. For this, Enyo needs notification access, which its setup asks for.\n\nAt the top, chat heads show the people you talked to most recently; a dot means unread messages. Below them come all your conversations, then your notes and to-dos, and finally your other notifications.</string>
    <string name="guide_chats_title">Conversations</string>
    <string name="guide_chats_body">Tap a conversation to open it and reply. When you write with the same person in several apps, Enyo shows those chats together, and you pick which app sends your reply.\n\nApps that support UnifiedInbox share whole chats with Enyo: photos, voice messages, files, places, reactions and ticks that show when a message was received and read. Touch and hold a message to react, reply to it or copy it. A conversation’s options let you link it to a contact, merge people, or unmerge them again.</string>
    <string name="guide_banners_title">Notification banners</string>
    <string name="guide_banners_body">New notifications appear as a short banner at the bottom of Enyo. Tap a banner to open all notifications of that app, or all messages, right where you are.\n\nWith your permission, Enyo’s banners can also appear over other apps. You can turn this on or off in Settings.</string>
    <string name="guide_quick_title">Quick controls</string>
    <string name="guide_quick_body">Quick holds what you change often: internet, Bluetooth, media volume, brightness and the flashlight, together with what is playing right now. From there, you can also open Android’s own quick settings.</string>
    <string name="guide_ai_title">Local AI</string>
    <string name="guide_ai_body">Local AI is optional. It stays off until you turn on AI features in Settings under System, and it runs entirely on your phone: nothing you ask leaves the device.\n\nAfter picking a model in Settings, ask it from Search. When you ask about them, it can look at your calendar, battery, weather or messages, and it remembers what you tell it about yourself. You can see and delete its memory in Settings at any time.</string>
    <string name="guide_data_title">Your data</string>
    <string name="guide_data_body">Enyo keeps your messages, notes and links in an encrypted archive on this phone. Under Backups in Settings, it writes encrypted copies to a folder you choose; with Syncthing, that folder can reach your other devices.\n\nSettings also lets you delete history, and its setup walks you through the permissions again whenever you like.</string>
